---

<ArticleLayout
    title={title}
    description={description}
    authorSlug={authorSlug}
    pubDate={pubDate}
    readingTime={readingTime}
    image={image}
    imageAlt={imageAlt}
    category="SaaS"
    categorySlug="tools"
    subcategorySlug="saas"
    headings={headings}
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <h2 id="introduction">Email Marketing Tools: Which One Actually Works?</h2>
  <p>
    Look, your email list is the only thing you actually own online. Instagram decides tomorrow they don't like your
    content? Gone. Twitter changes the algorithm? Your reach drops to nothing. That email list though? Yours. Export it,
    take it anywhere, nobody can touch it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Spent four months testing twelve different email platforms. Built lists, sent campaigns, set up automations. Tested
    everything from free newsletter tools to enterprise automation monsters.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quick answer if you're in a hurry: <a href="#convertkit">ConvertKit</a> for most creator stuff. The automation is
    powerful but the interface stays simple. Free up to ten thousand subscribers too. <a href="#beehiiv">Beehiiv</a>
    works if you're laser-focused on newsletter growth - monetization is built right in. <a href="#mailerlite">MailerLite</a>
    gives you the best free option with everything included.
  </p>

  <!-- Quick Picks -->
  <h2 id="quick-picks">My Top Picks</h2>
  <p>Short on time? Here's what I'd actually use:</p>
  <ComparisonTable
      columns={quickPicksColumns}
      rows={quickPicksRows}
      caption="Top 3 email marketing tools for creators and small businesses"
  />
  <p>
    All three let you start free. Test them. See which one feels right. Keep reading for the full breakdown of all eight
    platforms I actually recommend.
  </p>

  <!-- Why Email Marketing Matters -->
  <h2 id="why-email-marketing">Why Email Still Matters</h2>
  <p>
    Social media feels like where everything happens. Except email consistently crushes every other channel for actual
    results.
  </p>

  <h3>You Own Your List</h3>
  <p>
    Twitter followers, Instagram audience, LinkedIn connections. You don't own any of that. Platforms change algorithms.
    Ban accounts. Shut down. Your email list though? Yours. Export it anytime, move it anywhere.
  </p>
  <p>
    Matters if you're building a <a href="/guides/productivity">personal brand or remote business</a>. Email list is
    portable. Follows you through career changes,
    platform drama, all of it.
  </p>

  <h3>Better Engagement</h3>
  <p>
    Email open rates sit around twenty to forty percent. Social media organic reach? Under five percent usually. Email
    also converts at three to five times higher rates for sales.
  </p>
  <p>
    Average ROI for email marketing is thirty-six to forty-two bucks for every dollar spent. Highest of any marketing
    channel. If you're selling <a href="/tools/best-online-course-platforms">courses</a>, services, products - email is
    probably your main revenue driver.
  </p>

  <h3>Direct Access</h3>
  <p>
    Email lands in someone's inbox. Personal space they check multiple times daily. Unlike social feeds where your
    content competes with endless garbage, email gets actual attention.
  </p>
  <p>
    Direct line to your audience. Build deeper relationships. Share longer content. Guide people through journeys that
    would be impossible on social platforms.
  </p>

  <h3>Automation Works While You Sleep</h3>
  <p>
    Modern email tools let you create automated sequences that run without you. Welcome series. Educational sequences.
    Abandoned cart reminders. Re-engagement campaigns. All automatic.
  </p>
  <p>
    Well-designed automation nurtures leads for weeks or months. Turns cold subscribers into customers without any
    manual work after you set it up once. Essential for <a href="/guides/productivity">productive remote work</a>.
  </p>

  <!-- Detailed Reviews -->
  <h2 id="detailed-reviews">The Reviews</h2>

  <!-- ConvertKit -->
  <h3 id="convertkit">ConvertKit</h3>
  <p>
    Built specifically for <a href="/tools/best-productivity-apps">creators</a> - bloggers, podcasters, YouTubers, the
    whole content crew. It strips away
    complexity. Focuses on what actually matters. Growing a list. Tagging subscribers. Selling products.
  </p>
  <p>
    The automation builder just works? Intuitive but powerful somehow. Create sophisticated sequences without your brain
    melting. Subscriber tagging is probably best-in-class. Segment people based on interests, purchases, behavior,
    whatever you need.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's in there:</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    Visual automation builder for complex sequences. Drag-and-drop style.<br/>Tags for subscribers based on interests,
    purchases, engagement stuff.<br/>Landing pages and forms without needing your own website.<br/>Creator Network where
    you cross-promote with other creators.<br/>Sell digital products and subscriptions straight from it.<br/>High inbox
    placement rates consistently.<br/>Free up to ten thousand subscribers though automation is limited.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pricing:</strong> Free up to ten thousand subscribers but features get limited. Creator plan runs
    twenty-nine monthly for one thousand subs. Creator Pro fifty-nine monthly. Scales with your list size. Fourteen days
    free trial on paid tiers.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Using it myself:</strong> ConvertKit's interface feels simple after using bloated platforms like Mailchimp.
    Automation builder clicked for me immediately. Had welcome sequences running within an hour.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Good fit for:</strong> Bloggers and YouTubers and podcasters and <a
      href="/tools/best-online-course-platforms">course creators</a>. Anyone building an
    audience around content really. Especially valuable if digital products are your thing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's not great:</strong> Email templates are intentionally minimal. Great for deliverability, limited if
    your brand needs heavy design. Free tier restricts automation and key features. No built-in A/B testing on subject
    lines either.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/convertkit"
      text="Try ConvertKit Free . Up to 10K Subscribers"
      variant="primary"
      program="convertkit"
  />

  <!-- Beehiiv -->
  <h3 id="beehiiv">Beehiiv</h3>
  <p>
    Came from the team behind Morning Brew. Specifically designed for newsletter creators who want to grow fast and
    monetize. Includes features most platforms either charge extra for or straight up don't offer.
  </p>
  <p>
    The built-in referral program? Lets subscribers earn rewards for sharing your newsletter. Same growth hack Morning
    Brew used to reach millions. Ad network and paid subscriptions make monetization seamless.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>You get:</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    Referral program that gets subscribers to share and grow your list.<br/>Ad network for monetization without finding
    sponsors yourself.<br/>Paid subscriptions for premium newsletter tiers.<br/>SEO-optimized web hosting where each
    newsletter becomes searchable.<br/>Deep subscriber analytics and engagement tracking.<br/>Recommendations to
    cross-promote newsletters together.<br/>Free up to twenty-five hundred subscribers with most features.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Costs:</strong> Free up to twenty-five hundred subscribers. Launch plan thirty-nine monthly for ten thousand
    subs. Scale ninety-nine monthly for hundred thousand. Max three ninety-nine monthly for custom. Thirty days free
    trial on paid.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Using it:</strong> Beehiiv impressed me with its newsletter-first approach. Referral program setup took ten
    minutes. Saw measurable list growth from subscriber sharing within that first month.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Makes sense for:</strong> Newsletter creators focused on growth and monetization. Writers, journalists,
    <a href="/guides/freelancer-tax-deductions-guide">content creators</a> wanting to build a media business around
    their email list.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What bugs me:</strong> Less powerful automation than ConvertKit or ActiveCampaign. Platform is newer so some
    advanced features still cooking. E-commerce integration is limited compared to Mailchimp.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/beehiiv"
      text="Start Growing with Beehiiv . Free to 2,500 Subs"
      variant="primary"
      program="beehiiv"
  />

  <!-- Mailchimp -->
  <h3 id="mailchimp">Mailchimp</h3>
  <p>
    Most recognized name in email marketing. All-in-one platform going beyond email. Social scheduling, ads, postcards,
    e-commerce integrations, all of it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Brand recognition means excellent integrations. Virtually every tool connects to it. For e-commerce? The Shopify and
    WooCommerce integrations enable powerful abandoned cart and product recommendation emails.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>In the package:</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    Email, social, ads, everything in one platform<br/>Deep Shopify, WooCommerce, BigCommerce connections<br/>Visual
    automation builder for complex sequences<br/>AI-powered design suggestions<br/>Insights into subscriber behavior and
    lifetime value<br/>Website builder without code<br/>Free up to five hundred subscribers and one thousand monthly
    sends
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pricing:</strong> Free up to five hundred subscribers but limited. Essentials thirteen monthly for five
    hundred subs. Standard twenty monthly for five hundred subs. Premium three fifty monthly for ten thousand. Prices
    increase significantly as your list grows.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>My experience:</strong> Mailchimp's interface has improved but still feels cluttered compared to focused
    tools like ConvertKit. E-commerce features are excellent. If you don't have an online store though, might feel
    overwhelmed.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fits:</strong> Small businesses with e-commerce. Beginners who want all-in-one. Anyone needing extensive
    integrations with other tools.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's annoying:</strong> Pricing escalates quickly as your list grows. Free tier limited to five hundred
    subscribers (was two thousand). Interface can feel overwhelming with features you don't need.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/mailchimp"
      text="Try Mailchimp Free . Start Email Marketing Today"
      variant="primary"
      program="mailchimp"
  />

  <!-- ActiveCampaign -->
  <h3 id="activecampaign">ActiveCampaign</h3>
  <p>
    The power tool. If you need complex automation sequences, CRM integration, advanced segmentation - ActiveCampaign
    delivers capabilities simpler tools can't match.
  </p>
  <p>
    Automation builder is the most powerful I've tested. Create sequences that respond to dozens of triggers and
    conditions. Split paths based on behavior. Integrate with CRM data for truly personalized journeys.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's included:</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    Most powerful visual automation builder available<br/>Built-in CRM to track deals and relationships<br/>Site
    tracking to trigger emails based on website behavior<br/>AI determines best time to send to each subscriber<br/>Test
    up to five variations of emails<br/>Show different content based on subscriber data<br/>No free tier - fourteen-day
    trial, starts at twenty-nine monthly
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pricing:</strong> Lite twenty-nine monthly for one thousand subs. Plus forty-nine monthly includes CRM.
    Professional hundred forty-nine monthly for advanced features. Scales with subscriber count. Fourteen-day free
    trial.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>My experience:</strong> ActiveCampaign's learning curve is steeper. Automation capabilities worth it for
    complex businesses though. Built sequences that would be impossible in simpler tools.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Right for:</strong> Businesses with complex sales funnels. Agencies managing multiple clients. Anyone
    needing CRM integration with email marketing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The tradeoffs:</strong> No free tier. Fourteen-day trial is your only option to test. Interface can be
    overwhelming for beginners. Overkill for simple newsletter use cases.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/activecampaign"
      text="Try ActiveCampaign . 14-Day Free Trial"
      variant="secondary"
      program="activecampaign"
  />

  <!-- MailerLite -->
  <h3 id="mailerlite">MailerLite</h3>
  <p>
    Best balance of features and price for small creators and businesses. Free tier includes automation, landing pages,
    website builder. Features most competitors reserve for paid plans.
  </p>
  <p>
    Free plan is genuinely useful, not a crippled teaser. Get automation workflows, landing pages, pop-ups, twelve
    thousand monthly emails for up to one thousand subscribers. Paid plans start at just ten bucks monthly.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's included:</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    One thousand subscribers with automation included<br/>Clean intuitive email builder with drag-and-drop<br/>Landing
    pages without needing a website<br/>Website builder for simple sites<br/>Automation workflows on free plan<br/>Multiple
    form types for list building<br/>Shopify and WooCommerce supported
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pricing:</strong> Free up to one thousand subscribers with twelve thousand monthly emails. Growing Business
    ten monthly for five hundred subs. Advanced twenty-one monthly includes advanced features. Very competitive pricing
    at scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>My experience:</strong> MailerLite impressed me with how much you get for free. Interface cleaner than
    Mailchimp. Automation builder rivals paid tools. Best value in the market.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Ideal for:</strong> Budget-conscious creators. Small businesses just starting with email. Anyone wanting
    powerful features without paying premium prices.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The tradeoffs:</strong> Email templates more limited than Mailchimp. Approval process for new accounts can
    take twenty-four to forty-eight hours. Some advanced features require the higher-tier plan.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/mailerlite"
      text="Start Free with MailerLite . Full Features Included"
      variant="secondary"
      program="mailerlite"
  />

  <!-- Buttondown -->
  <h3 id="buttondown">Buttondown</h3>
  <p>
    Email marketing stripped to its essence. Want to write and send newsletters without dealing with complex features
    you'll never use? Buttondown's minimalist approach feels refreshing.
  </p>
  <p>
    Built for writers wanting to focus on content, not marketing automation. Write in Markdown, hit send, done.
    Simplicity is the feature.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>What's included:</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    Markdown support for plain text with formatting<br/>Clean interface without overwhelming dashboards<br/>Paid
    subscriptions to monetize with tiers<br/>Simple useful stats without complexity<br/>Custom domains for your
    links<br/>API access for developers who want to integrate<br/>Free up to one hundred subscribers
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Pricing:</strong> Free up to one hundred subscribers. Basic nine monthly for one thousand subs. Standard
    twenty-nine monthly for five thousand. Professional seventy-nine monthly for ten thousand plus.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>My experience:</strong> Buttondown is perfect if you find other tools overwhelming. Markdown-first approach
    appeals to developers and technical writers. Does one thing well.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Works for:</strong> Developers, technical writers, minimalists who want simple newsletter sending without
    marketing automation complexity.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The tradeoffs:</strong> Very limited free tier at one hundred subscribers. No visual email builder -
    Markdown only. No automation or landing pages. Not great for marketing-focused use cases.
  </p>
  <AffiliateButton
      href="/go/buttondown"
      text="Try Buttondown . Simple Newsletter Publishing"
      variant="ghost"
      program="buttondown"
  />

  <!-- Flodesk -->
  <h3 id="flodesk">Flodesk</h3>
  <p>
    Prioritizes aesthetics above all else. Brand depends on beautiful visuals? Want emails that look like they were
    designed by a professional? Flodesk delivers stunning templates with an intuitive builder.
  </p>
  <p>
